Below is a compact, self‑contained C# console program that shows the full “async/await” workflow.  
It does three things:

1. **Makes an asynchronous HTTP GET request** (`GetStringAsync`).  
2. **Parses the JSON response** asynchronously (using `Task.Run` to simulate CPU‑bound work).  
3. **Outputs the result** back to the console.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    // 1️⃣  An asynchronous method that downloads a page and parses a value from it.
    static async Task<string> FetchAndParseAsync(string url)
    {
        // a.  Create an HttpClient (ideally reused, but fine for a demo).
        using var httpClient = new HttpClient();

        // b.  Await the asynchronous network call.
        string json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);

        // c.  Offload CPU‑bound parsing to a background thread.
        string title = await Task.Run(() =>
        {
            using JsonDocument doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
            // For demo we assume the JSON has a property "title".
            return doc.RootElement.GetProperty("title").GetString();
        });

        return title;
    }

    // 2️⃣  The entry point – async Main is supported from C# 7.1 onward.
    static async Task Main()
    {
        // URL of a public JSON placeholder API that returns a post object.
        string url = "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1";

        try
        {
            // Await the async method. The rest of Main is non‑blocking.
            string title = await FetchAndParseAsync(url);
            Console.WriteLine($"Title of the post: {title}");
        }
        catch (HttpRequestException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Network error: {ex.Message}");
        }
        catch (JsonException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"JSON parse error: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}
```

### Why this example is good for learning

| Feature | What the code shows |
|---------|---------------------|
| **`async` keyword on a method** | `FetchAndParseAsync` declares that it may pause (`await`) without blocking the caller. |
| **`await` on an I/O operation** | `httpClient.GetStringAsync(url)` asynchronously fetches data from the network. |
| **`await` on a CPU‑bound task** | `Task.Run` pushes JSON parsing to a thread‑pool thread so the async flow can resume later. |
| **`async Main`** | Allows us to `await` inside the `Main` method; no need for `.Wait()` or `.Result`. |
| **Error handling** | `try/catch` around `await` shows how exceptions are propagated. |
| **No explicit thread management** | The runtime handles thread reuse and context switching. |

### Running the program

Compile with a recent .NET SDK (`dotnet build`) and run with `dotnet run`.  
You should see something like:

```
Title of the post: sunt aut facere repellat provident occaecati excepturi optio reprehenderit
```

Feel free to swap `url` for any REST endpoint you want to experiment with – the async pattern remains the same!
